# Rule-set compliance

- Follow all applicable project rules.
  Never silently violate a rule that applies to the current change.

- Apply rules according to their actual scope.
  When applicable rules overlap, prefer the narrower and more specific rule
  for the concern being addressed.

- Treat an explicit repository- or project-specific rule as an intentional specialization
  of a reusable generic rule when both govern the same concern.

- Do not infer precedence from filenames, directory ordering, or numeric prefixes.

- If applicable rules remain materially incompatible after considering their scope,
  surface the conflict rather than silently choosing one.

- Apply compliance checks proportionally to the change.
  Do not perform a repository-wide rule audit for a local edit
  unless the task or an explicit compliance workflow requires one.

- When an applicable rule is version-sensitive,
  follow the rule-set compatibility policy before assuming the guidance is current.

- If the repository provides an explicit rule-compliance skill or exception-recording workflow,
  use that mechanism when a rule violation must be intentionally accepted or deferred.
